<mujoco>
    <option gravity="0 0 -.5" />
    <worldbody>

        <!-- this creates the floor -->
        <geom
            type="plane"
            size="5 5 0.1"
            rgba="0.8 0.8 0.8 1"
        />

        <!-- creates a rigid body following x, y, z so x=0, y=0, z=.5 centered and .5 above in space -->
        <body
            pos="0 0 10"
        >
            <joint type="free" />

            <!-- the visible shape -->
            <geom
                type="box"
                size="0.1 0.1 0.1"
                solref="0.002 1"
                mass="1"
                rgba="1 0 0 1" />
        </body>
    </worldbody>
</mujoco>

<!--
worldbody: everything that lives in a world like ground, bodies, robot, objects. It's the top level
body that creates a collection of links and then connects then with joints that specify s child and
a parent link

body: defines the skeletal structure and kinematics tree of a model... geom elements give those
bodies physical dimensions, vol, and surface props.

body/joint: when a joint is defined inside a body it's function is not to connect the parent and
the child but rather to create motion degrees of freedom between them. If not joints are defined
within a given body, that body is welded to its parent

geom: reps a 3D shape ridgily attached to a body and is primarily used for collision detection,
visual rendering, and automatic mass/inertia calcs.
    - primitive types: plane, shpere, capsule, ellipsoid, cylinder, and box
    Anything more complex would use "mesh" (import 3D files) and "hfield" (height fields for rough
terrain
    - "size":
        - box: Half-lengths along the X, Y, and Z axes (3 values)
        - sphere: Radius (1 value)
        - capsule / cylinder: Radius and half-length (2 values)
    - pos / quat / euler: The position and orientation relative to the parent body's frame. By default,
MuJoCo aligns a geom's local origin exactly with its geometric center
    - rgba / material: Controls visual appearance (Red, Green, Blue, Alpha). Setting Alpha (the 4th
value) to 0 makes a geom invisible but keeps it physically interactive
    - friction: Defines contact mechanics (3 values: sliding, torsional, and rolling friction
coefficients)
    - contype / conaffinity: Bitmasks that dictate which geoms are allowed to collide with one another.



-->